IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `de-income-tax`

-- Delaware Code Title 30 ch. 11 -- worked cases from the seat of the resident taxpayer.
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Nothing here has been checked against a
-- Division of Revenue form or tax table. Expected results were worked by hand from the deposited text; the working is in the
-- comment above each assertion.

§ `Cases`

§§ `People and returns used below`

`a person aged 40` MEANS `Delaware person` OF 40, FALSE, FALSE, 40000, 0, 0, 0, TRUE, 10
`a person aged 62 on low earnings` MEANS `Delaware person` OF 62, FALSE, FALSE, 1000, 0, 0, 0, TRUE, 10
`a person aged 50 on low earnings` MEANS `Delaware person` OF 50, FALSE, FALSE, 1000, 0, 0, 0, TRUE, 10
`a person aged 63 on low earnings` MEANS `Delaware person` OF 63, FALSE, FALSE, 2000, 0, 0, 0, TRUE, 10
`nobody` MEANS `Delaware person` OF 0, FALSE, FALSE, 0, 0, 0, 0, TRUE, 0

GIVEN `type` IS A `Delaware return type`
      `who` IS A `Delaware person`
      `spouse` IS A `Delaware person`
      `fed AGI` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A `Delaware return`
`a 2025 return` `type` `who` `spouse` `fed AGI` MEANS
    `Delaware return` OF 2025, TRUE, `type`, `who`, `spouse`, FALSE, `fed AGI`, 0, 0, 0, NOTHING, 1, 0

§§ `s 1103 -- resident`

#ASSERT `Am I a resident of Delaware for income tax` TRUE FALSE TRUE 300
-- Domiciled here but within the 18-month foreign presence proviso: not a resident.
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I a resident of Delaware for income tax` TRUE TRUE FALSE 30)
-- Not domiciled; an abode here and 184 days: a resident under (2). 183 days: not ("more than 183 days").
#ASSERT `Am I a resident of Delaware for income tax` FALSE FALSE TRUE 184
#ASSERT NOT (`Am I a resident of Delaware for income tax` FALSE FALSE TRUE 183)

§§ `s 1161 -- who must file`

-- A resident single person not required to file federally, Delaware AGI $9,378: not "more than $9,378". $9,379: must file.
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I file a Delaware return` TRUE FALSE FALSE 9378)
#ASSERT `Must I file a Delaware return` TRUE FALSE FALSE 9379
-- A married resident entitled to file jointly, combined $15,449: no. $15,450: yes.
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I file a Delaware return` TRUE FALSE TRUE 15449)
#ASSERT `Must I file a Delaware return` TRUE FALSE TRUE 15450
-- Required to file federally: must file, whatever the income.
#ASSERT `Must I file a Delaware return` TRUE TRUE FALSE 0
-- A nonresident: s 1161(1) does not apply (s 1161(2), Delaware-source income, is not encoded).
#ASSERT NOT (`Must I file a Delaware return` FALSE TRUE FALSE 50000)

§§ `s 1162(a) -- joint or separate`

#ASSERT `Must we file separately in Delaware` `separate federal returns` EQUALS `they must file separate Delaware returns (s 1162(a)(1))`
#ASSERT `Must we file separately in Delaware` `a joint federal return` EQUALS `they may file either a joint or separate Delaware returns, as they elect (s 1162(a)(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Must we file separately in Delaware` `neither spouse must file a federal return` EQUALS `they may file either a joint or separate Delaware returns, as they elect (s 1162(a)(2), (3))`

§§ `s 1106(b)(3) -- pensions`

-- 2025, age 50, a $5,000 employer pension only: (b)(3)b.1.A, not over $2,000.
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2025 50 5000 0 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 2000
-- 2025, age 50, $5,000 pension and $10,000 military pension: the greater of $2,000 or $10,000 (B, up to $12,500).
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2025 50 5000 10000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 10000
-- 2027, age 50, $20,000 military pension: c.1.B caps it at $15,000. 2028: $20,000. 2029: $20,000 (cap $25,000).
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2027 50 0 20000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 15000
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2028 50 0 30000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 20000
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2029 50 0 20000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 20000
-- 2025, age 65, $25,000 pension and $5,000 eligible retirement income: b.2., not over $12,500.
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2025 65 25000 0 5000 TRUE 10 EQUALS 12500
-- 2025, age 65, $20,000 military pension: a pension "from ... the United States", so $12,500 under b.2.
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2025 65 0 20000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 12500
-- 2027, age 65, the same: the greater of $12,500 or the military limb, $15,000.
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2027 65 0 20000 0 TRUE 10 EQUALS 15000
-- Age 65, domiciled here before 2027 but only 2 years: f.4.A.I needs 3. Nothing.
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2025 65 20000 0 0 TRUE 2 EQUALS 0
-- Age 65, domiciled here from 2027: f.4.A.II needs 5 years. 4 years: nothing; 5 years: $12,500 (in 2032).
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2031 65 20000 0 0 FALSE 4 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much of my pension is subtracted` 2032 65 20000 0 0 FALSE 5 EQUALS 12500

§§ `s 1106(b)(2) -- the elderly or disabled on low income`

-- Single, aged 62, earned $1,000, federal AGI $9,000: $2,000 out. Delaware AGI $7,000.
#ASSERT `the Delaware adjusted gross income of` (`a 2025 return` `a single person's return` `a person aged 62 on low earnings` `nobody` 9000) EQUALS 7000
-- The same at federal AGI $10,500: over $10,000, no subtraction.
#ASSERT `the Delaware adjusted gross income of` (`a 2025 return` `a single person's return` `a person aged 62 on low earnings` `nobody` 10500) EQUALS 10500
-- Joint, both over 60, total earned $3,000, AGI $18,000: $4,000 out.
#ASSERT `the Delaware adjusted gross income of` (`a 2025 return` `a joint return of resident spouses` `a person aged 62 on low earnings` `a person aged 63 on low earnings` 18000) EQUALS 14000
-- Joint, only one over 60 (earned $1,000), AGI $9,000: $2,000 on this encoding's reading (NOTES.md finding 4).
#ASSERT `the Delaware adjusted gross income of` (`a 2025 return` `a joint return of resident spouses` `a person aged 62 on low earnings` `a person aged 50 on low earnings` 9000) EQUALS 7000

§§ `s 1108 -- standard deduction`

#ASSERT `What is my Delaware standard deduction` `a single person's return` 40 FALSE 0 FALSE FALSE EQUALS 3250
-- Single, 66 and blind: 3,250 + 2,500 + 2,500.
#ASSERT `What is my Delaware standard deduction` `a single person's return` 66 TRUE 0 FALSE FALSE EQUALS 8250
-- Joint, both 66: 6,500 + 2,500 + 2,500.
#ASSERT `What is my Delaware standard deduction` `a joint return of resident spouses` 66 FALSE 66 FALSE FALSE EQUALS 11500
-- Separate, aged 40, spouse 70 with no gross income and no one's dependent: 3,250 + 2,500 under (b)(2).
#ASSERT `What is my Delaware standard deduction` `a separate return of a married resident` 40 FALSE 70 FALSE TRUE EQUALS 5750
-- The same, but the spouse has income: 3,250.
#ASSERT `What is my Delaware standard deduction` `a separate return of a married resident` 40 FALSE 70 FALSE FALSE EQUALS 3250

§§ `s 1102(a)(14) -- the rates`

#ASSERT `What is the Delaware tax before credits` 1500 EQUALS 0
-- 2.2% of 3,000 = 66.
#ASSERT `What is the Delaware tax before credits` 5000 EQUALS 66
-- 66 + 3.9% of 5,000 (195) + 4.8% of 10,000 (480) + 5.2% of 5,000 (260) + 5.55% of 5,000 (277.50) = 1,278.50.
#ASSERT `What is the Delaware tax before credits` 30000 EQUALS 1278.5
-- To 60,000: 1,001 + 5.55% of 35,000 (1,942.50) = 2,943.50; + 6.6% of 40,000 (2,640) = 5,583.50.
#ASSERT `What is the Delaware tax before credits` 100000 EQUALS 5583.5

§§ `The balance`

-- Single, 40, wages $40,000, one exemption, no EIC, 2025: taxable 36,750; tax 1,001 + 5.55% of 11,750 (652.125) = 1,653.125;
-- less $110: 1,543.125.
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2025 TRUE 40 FALSE 40000 40000 0 0 0 0 10 1 0 EQUALS `answered` 1543.125
-- Single parent, 30, wages $15,000, two exemptions, federal EIC $4,000: taxable 11,750; tax 66 + 195 + 4.8% of 1,750 (84) =
-- 345; less $220: 125. Option a: 20% of 4,000 = 800, capped at 125: nil. Option b: 4.5% = 180, refundable: -55. b is better.
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2025 TRUE 30 FALSE 15000 15000 0 0 0 0 10 2 4000 EQUALS `answered` (0 MINUS 55)
-- Retiree, 67, federal AGI $50,000 with $20,000 Social Security, $25,000 pension, $5,000 eligible retirement income, here 10
-- years: 50,000 - 20,000 - 12,500 = 17,500 (no (b)(2): AGI over $10,000). Standard deduction 3,250 + 2,500 = 5,750; taxable
-- 11,750; tax 345; credits 110 + 110 (60 or over) = 220: 125.
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2025 TRUE 67 FALSE 0 50000 20000 25000 0 5000 10 1 0 EQUALS `answered` 125
-- The same retiree domiciled only 2 years: no (b)(3) subtraction; 30,000 - 5,750 = 24,250; tax 741 + 5.2% of 4,250 (221) =
-- 962; less 220: 742.
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2025 TRUE 67 FALSE 0 50000 20000 25000 0 5000 2 1 0 EQUALS `answered` 742
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2025 FALSE 40 FALSE 40000 40000 0 0 0 0 10 1 0 EQUALS `not covered` `the tax on a nonresident or part-year resident (subchapter III, s 1165) is not encoded`
#ASSERT `How much Delaware income tax do I owe` 2021 TRUE 40 FALSE 40000 40000 0 0 0 0 10 1 0 EQUALS `not covered` `only taxable years beginning on or after January 1, 2022 are encoded`

§§ `s 1117 -- which EITC option`

#ASSERT `Which EITC option is better` 125 4000 EQUALS `option b: 4.5%, refundable`
-- Tax $1,000, EIC $1,000: a leaves 800, b leaves 955. a.
#ASSERT `Which EITC option is better` 1000 1000 EQUALS `option a: 20%, not refundable`
-- Tax nil, EIC $2,000: a gives nothing; b refunds $90.
#ASSERT `Which EITC option is better` 0 2000 EQUALS `option b: 4.5%, refundable`

§§ `s 1168 -- filing and paying`

#ASSERT `By when must I file and pay in Delaware` 2025 EQUALS Date 30 4 2026
-- From 1 January 2026 (day 0) to 30 April 2026 (day 119). Filed on day 119: fulfilled.
#ASSERT EVALTRACE (`the s 1168 duty to file and pay` 2025) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the resident taxpayer` `file the return and pay the tax due on it` 119)) EQUALS FULFILLED
-- expect: BREACH BY the resident taxpayer, "return not filed or tax not paid by the thirtieth day of the fourth month ..." (day 130)
#TRACE (`the s 1168 duty to file and pay` 2025) AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the resident taxpayer` DOES `file the return and pay the tax due on it` AT 130
